-20"></a>Common types of API attacks</h2><p class=3D"">Cloud-native applica=
tions have amplified challenges in <a href=3D"https://www.wiz.io/academy/wh=
at-is-api-security"><u>API security</u></a>. When services depend on each o=
ther, a vulnerability in one API can create a cascade of security risks thr=
oughout the entire system. A good first step toward creating fail=E2=80=91p=
roof systems is to understand the common attack vectors.&nbsp;</p><p class=
=3D"">A go=E2=80=91to resource for learning more is <a href=3D"https://owas=
p.org/API-Security/editions/2023/en/0x11-t10/"><u>OWASP=E2=80=99s 2023 repo=
rt</u></a> on the top 10 <a href=3D"https://www.wiz.io/academy/api-security=
-risks">API security risks</a>. Below you=E2=80=99ll find common API attack=
 vectors that can arise from the risks OWASP outlines.</p><h3><div class=3D=
""></div><a id=3D"1-authorization-and-access-control-abuse-12" class=3D"rel=
ative -top-20"></a>1. Authorization and access control abuse</h3><p class=
=3D"">When an API accepts a request from an authenticated user without conf=
irming that the user has the right to perform the action or reach the resou=
rce, your systems are ripe for authorization and access control abuse. Inte=
rnal attackers or attackers with stolen credentials exploit these gaps to m=
ove beyond their intended privileges and gain sensitive data or access to s=
ystem functions.&nbsp;</p><figure><div data-rmiz=3D""><div data-rmiz-conten=
t=3D"not-found" style=3D"visibility: visible;"><div class=3D"relative overf=
low-hidden rounded-md sm:rounded-xl w-full shadow-md sm:shadow-xl" style=3D=
