ntrol abuse</figcaption></figure><p class=3D"">OWASP identifies three kinds=
 of authorization abuse:</p><ul><li><p class=3D"my-0!"><strong>Broken objec=
t-level authorization (BOLA):</strong> Attackers change an object ID in the=
 request path, query, or body. When the API fails to verify that the caller=
 owns that object, the attacker can view, edit, or delete someone else=E2=
=80=99s data.&nbsp;</p></li><li><p class=3D"my-0!"><strong>Broken function-=
level authorization (BFLA):</strong> A user calls an endpoint that performs=
 a privileged action, such as altering a GET request to DELETE. If the API =
does not enforce role and action checks, the user gains abilities meant for=
 administrators or other roles.&nbsp;</p></li><li><p class=3D"my-0!"><stron=
g>Broken object property level authorization (BOPLA):</strong> An endpoint =
returns or accepts updates to sensitive fields of an object, even though th=
e caller should not see or modify them. This exposes personal data and allo=
ws hidden tampering with protected attributes.&nbsp;</p></li></ul><h3><div =
class=3D""></div><a id=3D"2-authentication-attacks-17" class=3D"relative -t=
op-20"></a>2. Authentication attacks</h3><p class=3D"">Authentication attac=
ks bypass the mechanisms an API uses to confirm a caller=E2=80=99s identity=
. Attackers exploit missing rate limits, weak password policies, or poor to=
ken handling to log in as someone else and move freely through the system.&=
nbsp;</p><p class=3D"">These attacks are common because they are easy to ex=
ecute, and they carry severe consequences. For example, the <a href=3D"http=
s://cloudsecurityalliance.org/blog/2025/05/07/unpacking-the-2024-snowflake-=
data-breach"><u>2024 Snowflake data breach</u> involved credential stuffing=
 and stolen credentials obtained via malware to access customers' Snowflake=
 instances, resulting in financial losses of millions across multiple custo=
mers</a>.&nbsp;</p><p class=3D"">OWASP=E2=80=99s <a href=3D"https://owasp.o=
rg/API-Security/editions/2023/en/0xa2-broken-authentication/"><u>actionable=
 checklist</u></a> helps you determine whether your API or microservice is =
vulnerable to authentication attacks.</p><h3><div class=3D""></div><a id=3D=
"3-data-exposure-and-enumeration-21" class=3D"relative -top-20"></a>3. Data=
 exposure and enumeration</h3><p class=3D"">Without an up=E2=80=91to=E2=80=
=91date inventory of live APIs, versions, and hosts, businesses can fall vi=
ctim to data exposure and enumeration risks. During these attacks, simple s=
earch queries, DNS sweeps, and version fuzzing can identify exposed endpoin=
ts, extract data, or let malicious actors move deeper into the environment.=
</p><p class=3D"">Common data exposure and enumeration patterns include=E2=
=80=A6</p><ul><li><p class=3D"my-0!">Shadow or zombie endpoints that never =
made it into the official inventory&nbsp;</p></li><li><p class=3D"my-0!">Be=
ta or debug versions left reachable from the internet after testing; these =
builds often lack authentication or rate limits, making enumeration and bru=
te=E2=80=91force attacks easy&nbsp;</p></li><li><p class=3D"my-0!">Untracke=
d external data flows where sensitive fields travel to third=E2=80=91party =
APIs without visibility or controls, creating blind spots during incident r=
esponse</p></li><li><p class=3D"my-0!">Verbose error messages that reveal s=
tack traces, system paths, or object names, giving attackers detailed clues=
 for easier enumeration</p></li></ul><h3><div class=3D""></div><a id=3D"4-l=
ogic-abuse-25" class=3D"relative -top-20"></a>4. Logic abuse</h3><p class=
=3D"">Logic abuse is possible when an API functions exactly as designed fro=
m a technical perspective but fails to account for how automated interactio=
ns can manipulate legitimate business processes for malicious gain.</p><p c=
lass=3D"">Common logic=E2=80=91abuse patterns include=E2=80=A6</p><ul><li><=
p class=3D"my-0!">Skipping or reordering steps in a multi=E2=80=91step work=
flow to bypass validation or payment checks</p></li><li><p class=3D"my-0!">=
Reusing or stacking promo codes that were meant to be single=E2=80=91use, d=
raining marketing budgets</p></li><li><p class=3D"my-0!">Hammering sensitiv=
e endpoints with high=E2=80=91speed calls that lack proper rate limiting or=
 sequence enforcement</p></li></ul><p class=3D"">Logic abuse is most common=
ly leveled against financial services, e=E2=80=91commerce platforms, and e=
=E2=80=91wallets because these systems offer instant cash=E2=80=91out oppor=
tunities. For example, <a href=3D"https://www.fintechfutures.com/regulatory=
-actions/thieves-make-away-with-20m-from-revolut-following-payment-flaw"><u=
>Revolut=E2=80=99s 2024 refund=E2=80=91logic flaw</u></a> let attackers exp=
loit an automatic refund loophole and withdraw about=E2=80=AF$20=E2=80=AFmi=
llion.</p><h3><div class=3D""></div><a id=3D"5-denial-of-service-dos-or-dis=
tributed-denial-of-service-ddos-30" class=3D"relative -top-20"></a>5. Denia=
l of service (DoS) or distributed denial of service (DDoS)</h3><p class=3D"=
">When attackers send enough requests to drain CPU, memory, or bandwidth, a=
n API stops answering real users. These attacks are now so widespread that =
the terms =E2=80=9CDoS=E2=80=9D and =E2=80=9CDDoS=E2=80=9D have become part=
